<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       High-contrast overrides — merged over Tokens.axaml when the OS
       reports ColorContrastPreference.High, removed when it does not.
       See HighContrastThemeService.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

       WHERE THESE VALUES COME FROM, since "no hand-designed palette" was the
       instruction and this is the honest answer to it.

       Avalonia 11.3.18 does NOT surface the operating system's high-contrast
       palette. Verified against the shipped assemblies rather than assumed:
       PlatformColorValues exposes exactly ThemeVariant, ContrastPreference
       (NoPreference | High) and AccentColor1..3 — a PREFERENCE and an accent,
       no ButtonFace/WindowText/Hilight equivalents. There is nothing to copy.

       So these are not designed, they are DERIVED: pure black surfaces and pure
       white text and borders, which is the mechanical maximum (21:1) rather
       than an aesthetic choice, plus the smallest set of saturated hues needed
       to keep signals that differ by MEANING still differing by hue — error,
       warning and success stay distinguishable, as real HC themes keep them.
       Every pair is asserted against WCAG AA by HighContrastTokenTests; no
       value here is a matter of taste that a test does not check.

       The one thing genuinely lost is the OS's own accent. AccentColor1 IS
       available at runtime, but a static dictionary cannot hold a value that
       changes, and applying it would reintroduce a colour this file cannot
       verify the contrast of. Recorded as a limit rather than smuggled in. -->

  <!-- ── Surfaces: uniformly black, distinguished by border rather than fill ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WindowBackground" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelBackground" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBackground" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="InputBackground" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexHeaderBrush" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundListLowBrush" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundAltHighBrush" Color="#FF000000" />

  <!-- ── Transient states: never colour-only, and never brighter than the text
       that lands on them.

       These three were pure white until the rendered-pixel tests measured what
       is actually drawn on them. Inverting the FILL without inverting the TEXT
       erased both compositions the app has: a selected log row rendered
       LogTerminalForeground green on white at 1.37:1, and a pressed recent-file
       row rendered ForegroundSecondary white on white at 1.00:1 — invisible.
       The inversion could not be completed from here, because one of those two
       foregrounds is set in view markup (HomeView's caption) where no resource
       dictionary can reach it.

       So the fill is DERIVED from the three constraints it has to satisfy at
       once, rather than chosen: at least 3:1 against the black rest state
       (SC 1.4.11, the selection has to be visible AS a selection), and at least
       4.5:1 under both foregrounds that land on it — white text and the log
       terminal's #FF00FF00 green. Exactly eight greys satisfy all three,
       #5A5A5A through #616161; #5E5E5E is the one with the largest minimum
       margin, at 3.24:1 / 6.48:1 / 4.73:1. Guarded by
       HighContrastShippedDictionaryTests.

       DISCLOSED: this leaves selection only 1.62:1 from hover (#FF3F3F3F).
       They are told apart by persistence rather than by shade — hover follows
       the pointer, selection stays — and widening the gap would mean moving
       HoverBackground, which has consumers this change did not measure. ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HoverBackground" Color="#FF3F3F3F" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ActiveBackground" Color="#FF5E5E5E" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SelectedItemBackground" Color="#FF5E5E5E" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlHighlightListLowBrush" Color="#FF3F3F3F" />
  <!-- No consumer today (measured: no reference in any .axaml or .cs outside the
       token files), so this is a trap being closed rather than a bug being fixed
       — a future selection surface inheriting white would inherit 1.00:1 with it. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PropertyHighlightBrush" Color="#FF5E5E5E" />

  <!-- ── Text ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ForegroundPrimary" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ForegroundSecondary" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HeaderForeground" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseHighBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumHighBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumLowBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />

  <!-- Disabled text is the one deliberate exception, and it is an exception the
       standard grants: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.3 exempts "inactive user interface
       components". It still clears 4.5:1 on black here, so the exemption is not
       being leaned on — it is recorded because the value is intentionally the
       dimmest thing in the file. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ForegroundDisabled" Color="#FF949494" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseLowBrush" Color="#FF949494" />

  <!-- ── Accent and focus: white, because on a black ground nothing outranks it ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentPrimary" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentHover" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentPressed" Color="#FFC0C0C0" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemAccentBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderFocused" Color="#FFFFFF00" />

  <!-- ── Semantic hues: kept apart by HUE as well as luminance, because these
       three carry different meanings and must not collapse into one another ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentSuccess" Color="#FF00FF00" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentWarning" Color="#FFFFFF00" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccentError" Color="#FFFF6161" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningForeground" Color="#FFFFFF00" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningBannerBackground" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="WarningBannerBorder" Color="#FFFFFF00" />

  <!-- ── Compare-tab overlays ──
       The drop target loses its TINT and keeps its BORDER, which is this file's
       standing idiom: once every surface is black, structure is carried by the
       border rather than the fill. It also has to be, because the default
       theme's #400078D4 is a 25%-alpha blue that composites to roughly #001E35
       over a black pane — a fill that is present in the markup and invisible on
       screen. An opaque black fill with a white border says the same thing
       honestly, and the label on top goes from 3.86:1 over the composited tint
       to 21:1 on black.

       The scrim deliberately keeps its default value. A scrim's job is to dim
       what is behind it; under high contrast the surface behind it is already
       black, so there is nothing left to dim and the busy state is carried by
       the text and progress indicator drawn on top. Raising the alpha would
       change nothing a user can see. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DropTargetBackground" Color="#FF000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DropTargetBorder" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OverlayScrimBackground" Color="#80000000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OverlayForeground" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />

  <!-- ── Links ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HyperlinkForeground" Color="#FF00FFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HyperlinkHoverForeground" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />

  <!-- ── Borders: the primary structural signal once every surface is black ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSubtle" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderMedium" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BorderSeparator" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelBorderBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusSeparatorBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelHeaderSeparatorBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />

  <!-- ── Hex view: four roles that must stay mutually distinguishable ── -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexOffsetForeground" Color="#FFFFFF00" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexBytesForeground" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexAsciiForeground" Color="#FF00FFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LogTerminalForeground" Color="#FF00FF00" />

  <!-- Highlights stay translucent so the glyph under them survives; alpha is
       raised from the default theme's so the band is visible against black. -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexSelectionBrush" Color="#80FFFF00" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexMatchHighlightBrush" Color="#80FF8C00" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HexDiffHighlightBrush" Color="#FF7A0000" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="DiffRowBackground" Color="#66FF6161" />

</ResourceDictionary>
